msgid "Edit many units at once"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Change a field on several units in one step.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With several nodes selected, an edit goes to the active node, the last "
"one you clicked. Holding one key while you confirm the edit sends it to "
"every selected node that has that field. The key is :kbd:`⌘` on macOS and"
" :kbd:`Alt` on Windows and Linux. The change is one step of ``Edit ▸ "
"Undo`` and one line in the Log."
msgstr ""

msgid "Select the units"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the Matrix, click a unit, then :kbd:`⇧`-click or :kbd:`⌘`-click "
"(:kbd:`Ctrl`-click on Windows and Linux) the others, or drag a rectangle "
"on the empty canvas around them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Right-click a group and choose **Select the members** to select what it "
"holds."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The last unit you clicked is the active one: the **Inspector** shows its "
"fields."
msgstr ""

msgid "Apply a field to the whole selection"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Hold :kbd:`⌘` (:kbd:`Alt`). Beside every field that can be applied to the"
" selection, the Inspector says how many nodes it will reach, for example "
"**Apply to 5 selected nodes · 0 skipped**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With the key held, confirm the edit: press :kbd:`Enter` in a field, click"
" a button, choose in a menu, or release a slider."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For a text, write it in the active node's field and press :kbd:`⌘Enter` "
"(:kbd:`Alt+Enter`). On a field you have not changed, the same keys apply "
"the active node's value to the others."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The nodes that do not have the field are left as they are and counted as "
"skipped. The Log records the field and the number of nodes, for example "
"**«Description» applied to 5 selected nodes (0 skipped, without the "
"field)**. The selection stays as it was, so that you can go on with the "
"next field."
msgstr ""

msgid "The fields that can be applied in this way are:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"in the **Inspector**: the description, the colour, the value of a "
"property and the fields of its vocabulary, the start and end of an epoch,"
" the phase of a unit (for units of the same epoch), the rights of "
"resources and lots (licence, embargo, author), and the **×** that empties"
" a field;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"in the panels the Inspector opens: the epoch of creation of a document, "
"the type of end of a continuity node, and **End in…** or **Survives "
"until…** of a unit;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"in a **Tabular** window: every cell you can edit, such as the first epoch"
" of a unit in the **Units** sheet. The edit reaches the rows of that "
"sheet whose nodes are selected."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The name and the identifier of a node, and its type, are changed one node"
" at a time: the name is the identity of the node, and the type decides "
"which fields and relations it has."
msgstr ""

msgid "Move a selection to another epoch"
msgstr ""

msgid "Select the units, in the Matrix or in the Outliner."
msgstr ""

msgid "Press :kbd:`M`, or right-click and choose **Move to epoch… (M)**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Choose the epoch. The menu shows the plan before writing it: how many "
"nodes change, which follow their container, which are left out and why "
"(an epoch, a node already there, a node that follows its document or its "
"unit)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Confirm with **Move** followed by the number of nodes. The units now "
"belong to that epoch only, and the message offers **Undo**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Extend to epoch… (Alt+M)** (:kbd:`⌥M` on the Mac) keeps the units where"
" they are and makes them live in the chosen epoch too. In the menu, "
"**Move instead (M)** and **Extend instead (Alt+M)** switch between the "
"two."
msgstr ""

msgid "Bring old spellings of the relations up to date"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Files written by older tools may name some relations with an older "
"spelling, for example **Is bonded to** for **Bonded to**. EMStudio reads "
"them, and can rewrite them in the whole graph at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choose ``Edit ▸ Clean up ▸ Normalise spellings…``."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The window lists how many relations are written under an older spelling, "
"and what will happen to them: exact duplicates of a relation that already"
" exists are removed, and the others are rewritten under their current "
"name, with their data."
msgstr ""

msgid "Press **Normalise**. One undo takes it all back."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The same description, date, epoch or right on every unit that needs it, "
"written in one step and undone in one step."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`/reference/inspector` for the fields of each kind of node."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`check-the-chronology` for the dates of the epochs."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`filter-the-graph` to see only the units you want to select."
msgstr ""

#~ msgid "Choose ``Tools ▸ Normalise spellings…``."
